          My project 2 consists of an assortment of different techniques and deliberate choices. I wanted to create a sort of pong effect, like have shapes
bounce off the walls. On top of this, I really wanted to make some sort of staircase kind of look. I was inspired by the staircases in Squid Games,
which I believe are actually based off another art piece.
         To achieve this, I knew I had to have my shapes constantly moving. From a previous coding class, we learned how to use different speed 
variables(dx and dy) to increment the x and y values constantly so every shape redrawn would give the appearance as if it was moving. I applied 
this same logic with my piece, and told the computer that whenever an x or y value reached the edge of the canvas, just multiply the speed variable 
by -1 to change the direction to give the appearance that it was bouncing off the wall. I deliberately choose not to redraw the background after each 
rectangle was drawn, so every rectangle left a trail that looked like a staircase. 
         The random choices are shown at the very start. Overall, the starting position of the rectangles(the X and Y), the length/width, the speeds,
and color choice, are all random. Basically, I made an array to fill each slot with different random information that corresponds to a specific rectangle.
Pretty much each slot, like rects[3], for example, acts as an object, and within rects[3], there’s a random X value called x - so I could just 
use rects[3].x to tell the computer where to put the starting x value. This same idea applies to all other pieces of information stored
within their rects[] spot. I also included a random color number, and later on when drawing, did a simple if statement and if that
rectangle got a specific number in that random call, then it would be filled with this specific color. Like if the random number was 1, then 
draw pink, if it was 2, draw blue, etc.
	     The for loops were used well too! The first one was used to create those 30 slots in the rects array with random information values. The
second for loop was used to go through every single slot in rects, grab the infomation, adjust the x/y values via dx or dy, and draw the rect.
